VoxSpend Team Roles - Step by Step Explanation (No Code)
Understanding the Team Structure
Think of building a house:

Frontend Team = Architects and Interior Designers (decide how it looks)

Backend Team = Engineers and Plumbers (make it work properly)

Storage Developer = Storage Room Manager (keeps everything organized)

PART 1: FRONTEND TEAM (2 Members)
Member 1: UI Screens Developer
Role: "The Visual Designer"

In Simple Words: This person builds everything the user sees on the screen. If the app were a website, this person designs every page, every button, every form, and makes it look beautiful.

Step by Step What They Do:

Week 1: Setting Up the Foundation

Day 1-2: Open Qt Creator and create a new project. This is like opening a blank canvas.

Day 3: Design the main window - this is the first screen users see when they open the app. They decide where the title goes, where the menu is, and what color scheme to use.

Day 4-5: Design the sidebar navigation menu. This is the list of options like "Dashboard", "Add Expense", "Budget", etc.

Week 2: Building Each Screen

Day 1-2: Build the Dashboard screen - this shows a summary. They add boxes that will display total money spent, budget remaining, and small graphs.

Day 3-4: Build the Add Expense form - this is like a digital receipt. They add text boxes where users type "Lunch" as description, a box to type amount like "12.50", a calendar to pick date, and a dropdown menu to select category like "Food" or "Travel".

Day 5: Build the Edit Expense form - similar to Add Expense but with pre-filled information for when users want to change something.

Week 3: Finishing All Screens

Day 1-2: Build Budget screen - this shows how much money is left for each category. They add colorful progress bars that fill up as you spend more.

Day 3-4: Build Reports screen - this shows charts and graphs. They add pie charts showing "You spent 40% on Food, 25% on Travel..." and bar charts showing monthly spending.

Day 5: Build History screen - this shows all past expenses in a scrollable list, like a bank statement.

Week 4: Making It Beautiful

Day 1-2: Add colors, fonts, and icons. Make sure everything looks professional and consistent.

Day 3: Add animations - like when you click a button, it smoothly transitions to the next screen.

Day 4-5: Polish everything. Make sure buttons are big enough to click, text is readable, and screens look good on different screen sizes.

What They DON'T Do:

They don't write code that calculates totals

They don't save data to files

They don't check if you're spending too much

How They Work With Others:

They give their .ui files to Member 2 (UI Logic Developer) who connects the buttons to actual functionality

They ask the backend team what information needs to be shown on each screen

Member 2: UI Logic & Design Developer
Role: "The Bridge Builder"

In Simple Words: This person is the middleman. They connect the beautiful screens (made by Member 1) to the actual working parts (made by the backend team). They make sure when you click "Add Expense", the app actually saves your expense.

Step by Step What They Do:

Week 1: Planning the Connection

Day 1-2: Study all the screens made by Member 1. Understand what each button is supposed to do.

Day 3: Create a master plan of how everything connects. Draw a diagram showing: "When user clicks Add Expense → Get data from form → Send to backend → Show success message"

Day 4-5: Define the rules for all forms. For example: "Amount cannot be negative. Description cannot be empty. Date must be valid."

Week 2: Creating Controllers
Think of controllers as traffic cops directing where information goes:

Day 1-2: Create the main controller that manages the whole app. This decides which screen to show when you click different menu items.

Day 3: Create the expense controller. This handles everything related to expenses:

When you click "Add Expense", it collects all the data from the form

Checks if data is valid (no negative amounts, etc.)

Sends the data to the backend team's code

Shows success or error message

Day 4: Create the budget controller. This handles budget-related actions:

When you set a budget, it sends it to the backend

When you view budget status, it asks the backend for spending data

Day 5: Create the report controller. This handles:

When you click "Generate Report", it requests data from backend

Formats the data into understandable charts

Week 3: Connecting Everything

Day 1-2: Connect all buttons to their actions. This is like wiring a light switch to a bulb:

When you click "Add Expense" button → it triggers the controller

When you click "View Reports" → it opens the reports screen

Day 3-4: Make the app update automatically. When someone adds an expense:

The dashboard should automatically update the total

The history list should automatically show the new expense

The budget bars should automatically fill up more

Day 5: Add error handling. If something goes wrong:

Show user-friendly messages like "Please enter a valid amount"

Don't crash the app

Week 4: Testing and Final Polish

Day 1-2: Test every button, every form, every connection. Does "Add Expense" actually add? Does "Delete" actually delete?

Day 3-4: Make the app fast and responsive. If something takes too long, show a loading message.

Day 5: Review everything with the team. Make sure all connections work properly.

What They DON'T DO:

They don't design how screens look (Member 1 does that)

They don't write the actual expense calculations (Backend does that)

They don't save data to files (Storage Developer does that)

How They Work With Others:

They constantly talk to Member 1: "I need a button here. Can you add it?"

They constantly talk to Backend: "Your addExpense function needs to return success or failure so I can show the user"

They constantly talk to Storage: "After I save, tell me if it worked"

PART 2: BACKEND TEAM (2 Members)
Member 3: Core Expense Logic Developer (YOU)
Role: "The Brain"

In Simple Words: This person writes all the rules and calculations. If the app were a calculator, this person writes the math that happens when you press buttons. They don't care about colors or screens - they just care about making the numbers work correctly.

Step by Step What They Do:

Week 1: Creating the Data Structure
Think of this as creating a filing system:

Day 1-2: Design what an "Expense" looks like. Every expense has:

A description (like "Lunch at restaurant")

An amount (like $12.50)

A date (when you spent it)

A category (Food, Travel, etc.)

A unique ID number (like 1, 2, 3...)

Day 3: Create the list of categories. Decide what categories people can choose:

Food, Travel, Utilities (electricity/water bills), Shopping, Entertainment, Healthcare, Education, Other

Day 4: Define the rules for valid expenses:

Amount must be greater than 0

Description cannot be empty

Date cannot be in the future

Day 5: Test all these definitions. Make sure everything makes sense.

Week 2: Building the Expense Manager
This is like creating a smart filing cabinet that can organize and find expenses:

Day 1-2: Write the "Add Expense" function:

When someone gives you an expense, check if it's valid

Give it a unique ID number

Store it in your memory

Return "Success" if it worked, "Failure" if it didn't

Day 3: Write the "Edit Expense" function:

When someone says "Change expense #5", find it

Update the information with new data

Check if the new data is valid

Return success or failure

Day 4: Write the "Delete Expense" function:

When someone says "Remove expense #5", find and delete it

Make sure it's completely gone

Return success or failure

Day 5: Write the "Get All Expenses" function:

When someone asks, "Show me all expenses", give them the complete list

Return the list in a organized way

Week 3: Adding Calculation Features
This is where you make the app smart:

Day 1-2: Write the "Calculate Total Spent" function:

Go through all expenses, add up all the amounts

Return the total (like "You spent $350.50 total")

Day 3: Write the "Calculate by Category" function:

When someone asks "How much spent on Food?", add up only Food expenses

Do this for every category

Day 4: Write the "Get Monthly Report" function:

When someone asks "Show me spending for January 2024", find all expenses in January

Calculate totals by category for that month

Return a neat report

Day 5: Write search and filter functions:

Search by description (find all expenses with "lunch" in description)

Filter by category (show only Food expenses)

Filter by date range (show expenses from Jan 1 to Jan 31)

Week 4: Testing and Integration

Day 1-2: Test every function. Add 100 expenses, delete some, edit some. Make sure totals are always correct.

Day 3-4: Meet with other team members and make sure they can use your functions easily:

Give the UI Logic developer clear instructions: "Call addExpense() with these parameters"

Give the History developer a way to know when expenses change

Give the Storage developer a way to get all expenses for saving

Day 5: Final review and bug fixes.

What They DON'T DO:

They don't design screens

They don't connect buttons to actions (UI Logic does that)

They don't save to files (Storage does that)

They don't show messages to users (UI Logic does that)

How They Work With Others:

They give their functions to Member 2 (UI Logic) who calls them

They give their expense list to Member 5 (Storage) who saves it

They give expense changes to Member 4 (History) who tracks them

Member 4: History & Alarm Developer
Role: "The Recorder and Watchman"

In Simple Words: This person does two main things:

Keeps a diary of everything that happens (History)

Watches your spending and yells when you overspend (Alarm)

Step by Step What They Do:

Week 1: Building the History System
History is like a diary that records everything:

Day 1-2: Design what a history entry looks like:

What happened? ("Added expense: Lunch $12.50")

When did it happen? ("Jan 15, 2024 at 2:30 PM")

Who did it? (if multiple users)

What was the result? ("Successful" or "Failed")

Day 3: Write the "Add to History" function:

Whenever someone adds, edits, or deletes an expense

Automatically record what happened

Store it in order (latest first)

Day 4: Write the "Get History" function:

When someone asks "Show me what happened yesterday"

Return all history entries for that day

Day 5: Write the "Filter History" function:

When someone wants to see only "Added Expense" entries

Or only entries from the last week

Filter accordingly

Week 2: Building the Alarm System
This is like a guard dog that watches your money:

Day 1-2: Write the "Check Budget" function:

Ask the Expense Manager (Member 3) how much you've spent

Ask the Budget Manager what your limits are

Compare: "You set $200 for Food. You've spent $180. You have $20 left."

Day 3: Write the "Detect Overspending" function:

If spent amount > budget limit, trigger an alarm

Example: You spent $250 on Food when budget is $200 → ALARM!

Day 4: Write the "Trigger Alert" function:

When overspending is detected, create an alert message

"WARNING: You've exceeded your Food budget by $50!"

Send this message to be displayed on screen

Day 5: Write the "Check All Categories" function:

Check every category: Food, Travel, Utilities, etc.

Generate alerts for any that are over budget

Week 3: Adding Advanced Features

Day 1-2: Add notification settings:

Allow users to set alerts at 80% of budget (warning)

Allow users to set alerts at 100% (critical)

Example: "You've used 80% of your Food budget. Be careful!"

Day 3-4: Add budget monitoring:

Continuously track spending

When an expense is added, immediately check if any budget is broken

If broken, send alert to UI to show warning

Day 5: Add summary reports:

"This month you overspent on Food and Entertainment"

"You saved money on Travel"

Week 4: Testing and Refining

Day 1-2: Test with fake data. Add expenses until you break budgets. Make sure alarms trigger correctly.

Day 3: Test with real scenarios. What happens if user sets budget to $0? What happens if they spend exactly the budget amount?

Day 4: Make sure alerts are shown nicely. Work with UI team to make warnings look professional.

Day 5: Final testing and integration with all other parts.

What They DON'T DO:

They don't calculate totals (Expense Manager does that)

They don't save to files (Storage does that)

They don't show alerts on screen (UI Logic does that)

How They Work With Others:

They watch what Expense Manager does (Member 3)

They tell UI Logic when to show alerts (Member 2)

They tell Storage to save alerts (Member 5)

PART 3: DATA STORAGE TEAM (1 Member)
Member 5: Data Storage Developer
Role: "The Archivist"

In Simple Words: This person is responsible for saving everything so nothing is lost. When you close the app and open it again, all your expenses should still be there. They handle the saving and loading of all data.

Step by Step What They Do:

Week 1: Setting Up the Storage System
Think of this as setting up a filing cabinet with labeled folders:

Day 1-2: Set up the JSON library. JSON is like a simple text format that looks like:

text
{
  "id": 1,
  "description": "Lunch",
  "amount": 12.50,
  "category": "Food"
}
This is like writing information on index cards.

Day 3: Create the JSON Manager - this is the person who knows where everything goes:

Where to save expenses.json file

How to open it

How to close it properly

Day 4: Write the "Save Expenses" function:

Take all expenses from Expense Manager (Member 3)

Convert each expense to JSON format

Write all JSON to a file called "expenses.json"

Day 5: Write the "Load Expenses" function:

Open the "expenses.json" file

Read all the JSON data

Convert each JSON back into an Expense object

Give all expenses back to Expense Manager

Week 2: Adding More Storage Features

Day 1-2: Write the "Save Budget" function:

Take all budget settings (like "Food budget: $200")

Save them to a file (or same JSON file)

When app opens, load these settings

Day 3: Write the "Auto-Save" feature:

Whenever user adds an expense, automatically save to file

This way, if the app crashes, nothing is lost

Day 4: Write the "Save on Exit" feature:

When user closes the app, save everything

If auto-save is already working, this is extra safety

Day 5: Add file error handling:

What if the file doesn't exist? Create a new one.

What if the file is corrupted? Create a backup.

What if there's no space on the computer? Show a warning.

Week 3: Making Storage Robust

Day 1-2: Add backup system:

Before saving, make a copy of the old file

Name it "expenses_backup.json"

If anything goes wrong, you can restore from backup

Day 3-4: Add data validation:

When loading data, check if it's correct

Does each expense have all required fields?

Is the amount a number? Is the date valid?

Day 5: Add export/import features:

Export expenses to CSV (Excel format)

This allows users to open expenses in Excel

Import from CSV if users want to add many expenses at once

Week 4: Testing and Final Polish

Day 1-2: Test everything:

Add expenses, close app, reopen - are they still there?

Delete expenses, close app, reopen - are they gone?

Change budgets, close app, reopen - are the new budgets saved?

Day 3: Test error scenarios:

Delete the JSON file manually - does the app recreate it?

Put garbage text in JSON - does the app handle it gracefully?

Fill the hard drive - does the app show a warning?

Day 4: Optimize performance:

If you have 1000 expenses, saving should be fast

Loading should not freeze the app

Day 5: Final integration with all other members.

What They DON'T DO:

They don't calculate anything (Expense Manager does that)

They don't show screens (UI Screens does that)

They don't track history (History Manager does that)

How They Work With Others:

They get expenses from Expense Manager (Member 3) to save

They give expenses back to Expense Manager when loading

They save budget settings from Budget Manager

They save history from History Manager

PART 4: HOW EVERYONE WORKS TOGETHER
Real Example: Adding an Expense
Step 1: User Interaction

User opens the app (Member 1 designed this screen)

User clicks "Add Expense" button (Member 1 designed this button)

A form appears (Member 1 designed this form)

User fills in:

Description: "Lunch at Pizza Place"

Amount: "$15.50"

Date: "Jan 15, 2024"

Category: "Food"

User clicks "Save" button (Member 1 designed this button)

Step 2: UI Logic Takes Over (Member 2)

The "Save" button click is detected

Member 2's code wakes up and says: "I need to process this"

First, it checks: Is the description empty? No, it has "Lunch at Pizza Place"

Second, it checks: Is the amount positive? Yes, it's $15.50

Third, it checks: Is the date valid? Yes, Jan 15, 2024

Everything is valid! Now it creates an expense object with this data

Step 3: Core Logic Takes Over (Member 3 - YOU)

Member 2 calls your function: "Please add this expense"

Your code receives the expense

You give it a unique ID (like 45)

You store it in your internal list

You calculate: "Now total spent is $650.50"

You return "Success" back to Member 2

Step 4: History & Alarm (Member 4)

You tell Member 4: "I just added an expense"

Member 4 records in history: "Jan 15, 2024 at 2:30 PM - Added expense: Lunch at Pizza Place ($15.50)"

Member 4 checks budgets: "Food budget is $200. Spent so far is $180. After adding $15.50, spent is $195.50"

Member 4 checks: Is $195.50 > $200? No, so no alarm

Step 5: Storage (Member 5)

You tell Member 5: "I have new expenses, please save them"

Member 5 takes all expenses from you

Converts them to JSON format

Writes to expenses.json file

Returns "Saved successfully"

Step 6: UI Updates (Member 1 & 2)

Member 2 receives the "Success" from you

Member 2 tells Member 1's UI: "Show success message to user"

Member 1's screen shows: "Expense added successfully!"

Member 2 updates the dashboard:

Total spent increases from $635 to $650.50

Food category spending increases

Budget bar for Food fills up a little more

History list shows the new expense

Summary: Who Does What When Adding an Expense
Step	Who	What They Do
1	UI Screens	Shows the form, collects user input
2	UI Logic	Validates the input, prepares data
3	Core Logic (YOU)	Adds expense to memory, calculates totals
4	History & Alarm	Records what happened, checks budgets
5	Data Storage	Saves everything to file
6	UI Logic & Screens	Updates the display with new information
