PART 6: COMMON QUESTIONS
Q: What if I don't understand someone else's code?
A: Talk to them! Schedule a 15-minute meeting. Ask: "Can you walk me through your code?" This is normal in team projects.

Q: What if my code breaks someone else's?
A: This is why we have GitHub branches. Each person works in their own branch. We merge together after testing.

Q: How do I know what the others are doing?
A: Daily standup meetings. Every morning, everyone says what they're working on. Also, use a project board (like Trello or GitHub Projects) to track tasks.

Q: What if two people need to edit the same file?
A: This happens! In Git, when you try to pull someone else's changes, you might get a "merge conflict." You need to manually decide which changes to keep. The team lead usually helps with this.

Q: What if I fall behind schedule?
A: Speak up early! Tell your team: "I'm struggling with this part." They can help you or adjust the schedule.

Q: How much should I talk to the team?
A: More than you think! In school projects, students often don't talk enough. Send a message in the team chat daily. Ask questions. Share your progress.

PART 7: TEAM COMMUNICATION TIPS
Good Communication Habits
Daily Check-ins: "Today I'll work on X, I need Y from someone"

Ask Questions Early: Don't struggle for 3 days silently

Share Progress: "I finished the addExpense function, testing now"

Tell About Blockers: "I'm stuck because I can't figure out this error"

Be Specific: Instead of "It doesn't work," say "The addExpense function returns false even with valid data"

Document as You Go: Write comments in code so others understand

